model_answer:
The reflectivity r of a conducting layer between two dielectric media is given by the formula: \begin{equation}
r = \frac{\tan(\delta_1 - \delta_2) + \tan(\delta_1 + \delta_2) - \frac{2\alpha}{\sin(\theta_1 - \theta_2)}}{\tan(\delta_1 - \delta_2) + \tan(\delta_1 + \delta_2) + \frac{2\alpha}{\sin(\theta_1 - \theta_2)}}
\end{equation} where $\delta_1$ and $\delta_2$ are the phase changes of the incident and reflected waves, respectively, and $\alpha$ is a constant related to the conductivity and thickness of the conducting layer. This formula accounts for the reflection and transmission of electromagnetic waves at the interfaces between the dielectric and conducting media.

reference_answer:
The generalized law of refraction in 3-D is given by the equations:
\[
\begin{cases}
n_t \sin(\theta_t) - n_i \sin(\theta_i) = \frac{1}{k_o} \frac{d\Phi}{dx} \\
\cos(\theta_t) \sin(\phi_t) = \frac{1}{n_t k_o} \frac{d\Phi}{dy}
\end{cases}
\]
where \( n_t \) and \( n_i \) are the refractive indices of the transmitted and incident media, respectively; \( \theta_t \) and \( \theta_i \) are the angles of refraction and incidence, respectively; \( \phi_t \) is the out-of-plane deflection angle; \( k_o \) is the wavevector in vacuum; and \( \frac{d\Phi}{dx} \) and \( \frac{d\Phi}{dy} \) are the phase gradients along the x- and y-directions, respectively.
